{"number": 90, "text": "     “WA                                                                =\n\n                     Distance\n\n\n                                                            The hotel is a considerable distance from the beach.\n                      a considerable/long/short distance from\n                                                             [quite a long way]\n                                                             We have to live within commuting distance of my husband’s\n                      within commuting/walking distance\n                                                             office. [where it is possible to travel to work every day]\n                                                             On our cycling tour we managed to cover (a distance of) about\n                      cover/travel (a distance of) x kilometres\n                                                             40 kilometres a day.\n                                                             Zachary is always travelling to far-off/far-flung places. [distant]\n                      far-off/far-flung places\n                                                             I’ve never seen a member of the royal family at such close\n                      at close range\n                                                             range before. [so near]\n                                                             Many people who work here actually live in neighbouring towns.\n                      neighbouring town/country/area\n\n |            B|     Little, small  and   large\n             =          tall)                 Laws                   (NAT emall\\\n                     We talk about low prices, low wages, low levels. (NOT smat)\n                     We say small quantities, small numbers,  small amounts,  a small increase. (NOT title}\n                     The opposite is large quantities, large numbers, large amounts, a large increase.\n                     Similarly, we talk about problems or objects being on a large scale or on a small scale:\n                     If you are walking in the mountains you need a large scale map.\n                     The UK has similar problems to the USA, but on a smaller scale.\n                     In informal spoken English we often use little after another adjective to make it sound more\n                     friendly, e.g. poor little Joe, dear little dog, nice little room.\n                     Little can also mean young, e.g. little brother [informal: younger brother, NOT brother;\n                     the opposite is big brother]. Sometimes little or small suggests that something is not very\n                     important, e.g. a little problem,  got a lot of little things to do, to make someone look\n                     small, to make small talk [talk socially, about unimportant subjects].\n\n                     Other   size collocations\n                     We can talk about fat books and slim books as well as fat people and slim people. Only people\n                     (not books) can be plump or skinny or painfully thin.\n                     We use a great deal of (NOT      or big) in contexts like this:\n                     She should be able to help you because she has a great deal of time / a great deal of money /\n                     a great deal of energy / a great deal of enthusiasm.\n                     Major and minor  often collocate with words relating to problems or points in an argument, e.g.\n                     major/minor  difference, major/minor  change,  major/minor  effect, major/minor difficulty,\n                     major/minor  point, major/minor  issues, major/minor  factor.\n\n\n                     : Take c\n                              re with the different collocations that go with tall and high. We talk about tall people, tall\n                       trees, tall buildings, but high mountains, high prices, high interest rates, high heels, high tide,\n                       high jump. Make  a note of any collocations with tall or high as you notice them.\n                                                                                       Ss      =\n\n\n      88             English Collocations in Use Intermediate", "char_count": 4221, "mean_conf": 89.98, "word_count": 472, "words": [{"text": "a", "x0": 431, "y0": 598, "x1": 447, "y1": 621, "conf": 92, "bold": false}, {"text": "considerable/long/short", "x0": 460, "y0": 590, "x1": 877, "y1": 629, "conf": 91, "bold": false}, {"text": "distance", "x0": 888, "y0": 590, "x1": 1030, "y1": 620, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "from", "x0": 1040, "y0": 589, "x1": 1120, "y1": 620, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "The", "x0": 1194, "y0": 589, "x1": 1258, "y1": 619, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "hotel", "x0": 1270, "y0": 589, "x1": 1354, "y1": 619, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "is", "x0": 1368, "y0": 590, "x1": 1392, "y1": 619, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "a", "x0": 1413, "y0": 597, "x1": 1420, "y1": 619, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "considerable", "x0": 1432, "y0": 588, "x1": 1663, "y1": 619, "conf": 96, "bold": true}, {"text": "distance", "x0": 1674, "y0": 588, "x1": 1824, "y1": 618, "conf": 96, "bold": true}, {"text": "from", "x0": 1835, "y0": 588, "x1": 1914, "y1": 618, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "the", "x0": 1926, "y0": 588, "x1": 1981, "y1": 618, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "beach.", "x0": 1993, "y0": 588, "x1": 2104, "y1": 618, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "[quite", "x0": 1197, "y0": 638, "x1": 1285, "y1": 675, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "a", "x0": 1295, "y0": 649, "x1": 1310, "y1": 667, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "long", "x0": 1322, "y0": 641, "x1": 1388, "y1": 676, "conf": 70, "bold": false}, {"text": "way]", "x0": 1397, "y0": 637, "x1": 1467, "y1": 675, "conf": 90, "bold": false}, {"text": "within", "x0": 430, "y0": 708, "x1": 535, "y1": 73